      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 2,
      "text": "Before its modern founding, the area was home to the Saxapahaw Indians, whose presence is still remembered in local lore.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        1
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://townofossipeenc.com/about",
      "category": "people",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 3,
      "text": "The town’s roots trace back to 1878 when Williamson and Sons built the Ossipee Cotton Mill along the Haw River.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        3
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://townofossipeenc.com/about",
      "category": "economy",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 4,
      "text": "In the 1930s, Burlington Industries began selling mill houses to workers, a move aimed at preventing labor strikes.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        26
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://townofossipeenc.com/about",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 5,
      "text": "August 31, 1934, marked a dramatic day when armed robbers seized the mill office, though little money was actually taken.",
      "sourceSentenceIds": [
        28,
        29,
        30
      ],
      "sourceLabels": [
        "Web source 1"
      ],
      "source": "https://townofossipeenc.com/about",
      "category": "history",
      "quality": "good"
    },
    {
      "id": 6,
      "text": "Mill Point depot once served as the town’s shipping yard, linking Ossipee Cotton Mill to regional markets.",
